"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[1735],{1735:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-08","slug":"base-08","trackId":"base","order":9,"title":"Cryptocurrency Regulation","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":13,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-08"],"markdown":"> An educational review of regulation as described at the source date. The limits and proposed rules mentioned require verification against current law.\\n\\nLesson 8 \xb7 9 min\\n\\n## Regulation\\n\\nCryptocurrency Regulation in Russia\\n\\nPrevious lessons explained how cryptocurrency works, how to use wallets and the financial capabilities of DeFi. An obvious question follows:\\n\\nhow legal is all this in Russia?\\n\\nCan you freely buy and sell cryptocurrency? May you hold USDT in your own wallet? Must you pay tax and report transactions to the government? These questions matter particularly as Russia develops a new regulatory system for its crypto market. Start with the main point.\\n\\nCryptocurrency is not prohibited in Russia.\\n\\nIt can be owned, bought, sold and held in a personal wallet subject to legal requirements. Using it to pay for ordinary goods and services inside Russia is generally prohibited, with exceptions for some foreign-trade settlements. This lesson examines rules already in effect, changes described for 2027 and their implications for ordinary users.\\n\\n### 1. How the Government Regulates Cryptocurrency\\n\\nThe source describes Federal Law No. 282-FZ, “On Digital Currencies and Digital Rights,” as effective from 1 September 2026. Its central aim is a Russian regulated crypto market under Bank of Russia supervision. The stock market offers an analogy: shares are generally purchased through a state-regulated broker.\\n\\nA similar system is being formed for cryptocurrency, with several main participants.\\n\\nBroker\\n\\nBuys and sells cryptocurrency on your instructions.\\n\\nCrypto Conversion Service\\n\\nOrganizes exchanges between ordinary money and cryptocurrency.\\n\\nDigital Depository\\n\\nRecords clients’ crypto assets and facilitates legally prescribed operations. Organizations operating within the Russian regulated system must meet requirements and, where applicable, enter special Bank of Russia registers. But remember:\\n\\nthe new system did not become fully operational in a single day.\\n\\nDifferent requirements have different commencement dates.\\n\\n### 2. What Changed on 1 September 2026?\\n\\nThe main law governing the new crypto market took effect on that date according to the source. The government defined eligible market organizations, infrastructure arrangements and participant requirements. Implementation is gradual.\\n\\nTherefore, 1 September did not mean every crypto holder had to close foreign exchange accounts immediately or move assets to Russian organizations. Transaction rules will nevertheless change.\\n\\nParticularly important changes are scheduled for 2027.\\n\\nAdditional intermediary restrictions, transaction requirements and reporting duties are described as beginning then.\\n\\n### 3. What Happens to Foreign Exchanges?\\n\\nMany users are accustomed to Binance, Bybit, OKX and other foreign crypto exchanges. Must they close those accounts?\\n\\nMerely having a foreign exchange account does not automatically breach the law. But distinguish two things:\\n\\nowning cryptocurrency and transacting in it are different regulatory activities.\\n\\nAdditional requirements for residents’ crypto transactions are described as taking effect on 1 July 2027. The law envisages operations through specified market participants, with exceptions and special conditions for foreign operations. Do not assume every method of buying or selling on a foreign exchange will automatically remain permitted.\\n\\nRussian banks will also have to restrict certain payments connected with unlawful organization of crypto circulation. This may affect familiar ways of converting rubles to crypto. The practical question will concern not merely the exchange holding your assets, but legal methods of buying cryptocurrency, transferring money and withdrawing it.\\n\\nApplication of the new requirements will continue developing.\\n\\n### 4. What Happens to Personal Wallets?\\n\\nWe have already examined crypto wallets. There is good news:\\n\\nusing your own crypto wallet is not prohibited in itself.\\n\\nYou do not automatically have to transfer everything to a Russian depository or give anyone private keys. You can continue holding your assets yourself. But distinguish:\\n\\nholding cryptocurrency in your own wallet and performing operations through it.\\n\\nCustody may remain permissible while transfers, exchanges and other operations face legal requirements. Reporting duties for assets and operations outside Russian regulated infrastructure are also being introduced gradually. A personal wallet does not place its use entirely outside regulation.\\n\\n### 5. Must You Report Cryptocurrency to the Government?\\n\\nSeparate two questions:\\n\\nMust you pay tax?\\n\\nMust you report crypto transactions?\\n\\nThese are not the same.\\n\\n#### Taxes Already Apply\\n\\nTaxable income from selling cryptocurrency may create tax obligations. Suppose you buy Bitcoin for 1 000 000 rubles and later sell it for 1 300 000 rubles.\\n\\nYour profit is:\\n\\n1 300 000 − 1 000 000 = 300 000 rubles.\\n\\nWith documents proving acquisition costs, the taxable base in this simplified example is 300 000 rubles, not all 1 300 000 rubles received. Keep:\\n- purchase and sale histories;\\n- bank transfer records;\\n- conversion-service receipts and documents;\\n- exchange statements;\\n- fee records;\\n- wallet transaction histories.\\n\\nIf you declare crypto-sale income yourself, the 3-PIT declaration is generally due by 30 April of the following year, with tax payable by 15 July. Specific obligations depend on the operations and taxpayer status.\\n\\n#### New Reporting from 2027\\n\\nReporting for crypto outside Russian regulated infrastructure is a separate issue. The source describes new provisions effective from 2 May 2027 for operations through addresses not serviced by Russian digital depositories. They may cover:\\n- personal crypto wallets;\\n- operations through foreign infrastructure;\\n- crypto assets recorded abroad.\\n\\nResident individuals face applicable duties and exemptions. One exemption concerns certain citizens who spent more than 183 days outside Russia during the preceding calendar year. Additional regulations must define detailed reporting procedures.\\n\\nAt the lesson’s preparation date, it was therefore impossible to specify every form, deadline and reporting detail for every situation.\\n\\nDo not confuse income tax with transaction-reporting duties.\\n\\nAn operation producing no taxable profit does not necessarily eliminate other reporting duties.\\n\\n### 6. Restrictions for Ordinary Investors\\n\\nThe new Russian system divides investors into two main categories.\\n\\nNon-Qualified Investors\\n\\nOrdinary users without qualified-investor status.\\n\\nQualified Investors\\n\\nUsers meeting prescribed knowledge, experience, asset or other criteria. Non-qualified investors face additional restrictions.\\n\\n#### The 300 000-Ruble Limit\\n\\nThe source describes a Bank of Russia limit within the planned system:\\n\\n300 000 rubles of cryptocurrency purchases per calendar year through one intermediary for a non-qualified investor.\\n\\nFor example:\\n- you buy 100 000 rubles of Bitcoin;\\n- then 100 000 rubles of USDT;\\n- then another 100 000 rubles of ETH.\\n\\nTotal purchases reach 300 000 rubles and exhaust the allowance. Selling previously acquired crypto does not reset accumulated purchases. Access to operations also involves testing users’ understanding of crypto market risks.\\n\\nImportant: Bank of Russia regulations determine implementation details. In October 2026, Direction No. 7441-U was still undergoing state registration according to the source.\\n\\nLimit: 300 000 ₽ annually through one intermediary. How much remains? Annual purchases 0 ₽ / 300 000 ₽. You can buy another 300 000 ₽.\\n\\nThe Bank of Russia determines exactly how the allowance is calculated. Check the rules.\\n\\n#### Which Cryptocurrencies Will Be Available?\\n\\nNon-qualified investors in the regulated Russian system will not have access to every cryptocurrency. The Bank of Russia plans a list of highly liquid assets with sufficiently long trading histories. The initial list described includes:\\n- Bitcoin (BTC);\\n- Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) (ETH);\\n- Tether ↗ (opens in a new tab) (USDT).\\n\\nCheck the final applicable list against Bank of Russia regulations. Qualified investors have broader access, but their status does not remove legal requirements or platform rules.\\n\\n### 7. Changes Expected in 2027\\n\\nHere are the principal dates in one place.\\n| Date | Change |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 2 May 2027 | New reporting provisions for crypto operations outside Russian digital depositories begin |\\n| 30 June 2027 | A significant part of the transition period ends |\\n| 1 July 2027 | Additional crypto transaction requirements and restrictions on unlawful intermediaries take effect |\\n| 1 September 2027 | Additional safeguards apply to certain transfers through Russian regulated infrastructure |\\n\\n#### Additional Transfer Delays\\n\\nAdditional anti-fraud measures are described for 1 September 2027. Certain crypto transfers through Russian digital depositories will face mandatory delays. Under specified conditions, a transfer to an external address exceeding 100 000 rubles must wait at least 48 hours.\\n\\nSeparate thresholds apply to some transfers to third parties. Why? Suppose fraudsters convince someone to transfer all their cryptocurrency urgently. An instant transfer is almost impossible to reverse.\\n\\nA mandatory delay gives time to detect fraud and try to stop the operation, similar to a banking cooling-off period. But remember:\\n\\nthis does not mean every OKX Wallet ↗ (opens in a new tab) transfer will be delayed by 48 hours.\\n\\nThe rules concern specified operations through regulated Russian infrastructure.\\n\\n#### What About Futures?\\n\\nCrypto activity includes derivatives such as futures, not merely purchases of underlying assets. Separate requirements may apply. Restrictions on buying cryptocurrency cannot automatically be extended to every trading operation. A later lesson examines exchanges and trading risks.\\n\\n### 8. The Essentials\\n\\nSet aside legal complexity and remember several things. Cryptocurrency is not prohibited in Russia. Subject to prescribed rules, it can be owned, bought, sold and held in personal wallets. The source describes a new regulatory framework effective from 1 September 2026, setting intermediary requirements and gradually forming a regulated market.\\n\\nAdditional 2027 requirements concern transaction procedures, reporting and certain transfers. Taxes already apply. Taxable crypto-sale income requires considering tax obligations and retaining supporting documents.\\n\\nPersonal wallets remain a permissible custody method, but this does not exempt every operation through them from regulation. Above all:\\n\\nthe law governs both cryptocurrency and the methods used to buy, sell and transfer it.\\n\\nUnderstand not only blockchain technology, but the rules in the country where you transact. The next practical question is:\\n\\nhow can you buy cryptocurrency for ordinary rubles without encountering scammers, freezes or other problems?\\n\\nThe exchange rate is not the only issue. Consider whom you pay, where assets originate and whether an exchange will later accept them. Trying to save 0,5% on conversion can cause much larger losses.\\n\\nThe next lesson explains safe purchases, verification of platforms and counterparties, and common errors when converting rubles to digital assets.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhich of the following is generally permitted in Russia?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Broker** — An intermediary through which you place exchange orders. With leverage, it adds its money to yours and wants it back regardless of whether the market has fallen\\n- **Personal Income Tax** — 13% of annual income up to 5 million ₽, and 15% above that threshold. An employer pays it before the money reaches you\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Regulated Price** — A price set by the government rather than the market: gas, electricity, water and public transport. Changes follow a schedule and a known percentage, allowing households to budget for the increase\\n- **Futures Contract** — An agreement to buy or sell something in the future at a price agreed today. Futures prices show market expectations, from oil prices to Federal Reserve interest-rate decisions. These are expectations, not forecasts; the market is regularly wrong\\n- **Private Key** — What allows you to control the cryptocurrency at an address. The crypto itself is recorded on the blockchain; the wallet stores the keys, which are what you need to protect\\n- **Qualified Investor** — A status for people who have demonstrated knowledge, experience or capital. Ordinary, non-qualified investors have access to fewer instruments and face limits\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: Which of the following is generally permitted in Russia?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Freely paying for any goods in Russian shops with cryptocurrency\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Using cryptocurrency only after giving private keys to the government\\n\\n**Question 2: Does the new regulation automatically require closing MetaMask, Ledger or a foreign exchange account?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Yes, all such wallets become prohibited\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited\\n- ⚪ Option 3: You may keep Ledger only, but must close MetaMask\\n\\n**Question 3: Are tax on crypto-sale profits and transaction reporting the same obligation?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Yes, no tax means reporting can never be required\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: No, they are different obligations\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Reporting applies only to miners\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-08-1","prompt":"Which of the following is generally permitted in Russia?","options":[{"id":"base-08-1-1","label":"Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law"},{"id":"base-08-1-2","label":"Freely paying for any goods in Russian shops with cryptocurrency","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law"},{"id":"base-08-1-3","label":"Using cryptocurrency only after giving private keys to the government","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law"}],"explanation":"Owning, buying, selling and holding cryptocurrency in compliance with the law","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1756–1759 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-08-2","prompt":"Does the new regulation automatically require closing MetaMask, Ledger or a foreign exchange account?","options":[{"id":"base-08-2-1","label":"Yes, all such wallets become prohibited","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited"},{"id":"base-08-2-2","label":"No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited"},{"id":"base-08-2-3","label":"You may keep Ledger only, but must close MetaMask","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited"}],"explanation":"No, merely owning these wallets and accounts is not prohibited","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1761–1764 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-08-3","prompt":"Are tax on crypto-sale profits and transaction reporting the same obligation?","options":[{"id":"base-08-3-1","label":"Yes, no tax means reporting can never be required","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No, they are different obligations"},{"id":"base-08-3-2","label":"No, they are different obligations","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. No, they are different obligations"},{"id":"base-08-3-3","label":"Reporting applies only to miners","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No, they are different obligations"}],"explanation":"No, they are different obligations","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1766–1769 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":1530,"lineEnd":1774}}')}}]);